# Ansible Notes


## Configuring Password-Less Setup
Not literally Password less we will create ssh keys and add configuration in ansible to use the secret key generated.

```
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -a 100 -f ~/.ssh/my-server-key -C "prod-server"
```

Copy the public key and add it to the Authorized keys file in the target server.

## Configure Ansible

```shell
python3 -m pip install ansible
```

Note: If you dont have pip, install it first

## First Steps
### Creating a inventory file

- You can create file in both ini format and yaml
- I prefer yaml

Here there is a format which needs to be followed for the inventory file. do check that.

```yaml
all:
    vars:
    children:
        [group]:
            vars:
            hosts:
                [your own hostname]
```

Sample
```yaml

all:
  children:
    webservers:
      vars:
        ansible_ssh_private_key_file : /home/ec2-user/.ssh/my-server-key
        ansible_user: ec2-user
      hosts:
        host1:
          ansible_host: 172.31.20.235
```

To verify your inventory file

```shell
ansible-inventory -i inventory.yaml --graph
ansible-inventory -i inventory.yaml --list # this more nicer
```

### Adhoc Commands

You can check more on the official website
https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/command_guide/intro_adhoc.html

```shell
ansible -i inventory.yaml -m ping all
ansible -i inventory.yaml -m shell -a "ls /" all
```


## Now lets see about playbooks

A Playbook is a YAML file that defines a series of actions to be executed on managed nodes. It contains one or more "plays" that map groups of hosts to roles

- Sample Playbook
```yaml
---

- hosts: webservers
  become: y
  tasks:
    - name: Install apache httpd
      ansible.builtin.yum:
        name: httpd
        state: present
        update_cache: yes
    - name: copy the sample file
      ansible.builtin.copy:
        src: ./index.html
        dest: /var/www/html
        owner: root
        group: root
        mode: '0644'
    - name : Start the service
      ansible.builtin.service:
        name: httpd
```

```shell
ansible-playbook -i inventory.yaml httpd-play/apache-play.yaml
```

## Roles

### Ansible Galaxy

So now, so far we played around and created plabook & adhoc commands, but if you got more tasks to be run and you want to reuse it this will get messy to be in a single file. 

To help with that we have Roles. It provides sttuctural way to maintain all these

```shell
ansible-galaxy role init httpd-test
```
The above command creates you a skeleton sturcture for the role

```
httpd-test
├── README.md
├── defaults
│   └── main.yml
├── files
├── handlers
│   └── main.yml
├── meta
│   └── main.yml
├── tasks
│   └── main.yml
├── templates
├── tests
│   ├── inventory
│   └── test.yml
└── vars
    └── main.yml

8 directories, 8 files
```

Deafaults ----> Are default variables which can be used accross the role

Files ----> You static content files

Handlers ---> Are kind of function, you can call them repeatedly based on action

meta ----> Metadata about the role

tasks ----> Your list of tasks

templates ----> You use templates(jinja2) when you want to dynamically edit a file based on run time and input vars

vars ----> This is the variables of your role


#### Now lets restructure our previous playbook into role

```yaml
# main.yaml

- hosts: webservers
  become: y
  roles:
    - httpd-test
```

```yaml
# httpd-test/tasks/main.yaml

---
# tasks file for httpd-test

- name: Install apache httpd
  ansible.builtin.yum:
    name: httpd
    state: present
    update_cache: yes
- name: copy the sample file
  ansible.builtin.copy:
    src: files/index.html
    dest: /var/www/html
    owner: root
    group: root
    mode: '0644'
  notify:
    - restart_httpd
```

```yaml
# httpd-test/handlers/main.yaml
---
# handlers file for httpd-test
- name : start_httpd
  ansible.builtin.service:
    name: httpd
    state: started
- name : restart_httpd
  ansible.builtin.service:
    name: httpd
    state: restarted
```